// A `match` over a declared type becomes a test chain, and the text shows every
// part of it. The discriminator is read ONCE (`tag int v0`) because the build
// compares one register against every arm (`buildMatchExpr`), each test names the
// variant's declared position as the number the C backend will define as
// `Color_TAG_Red` (`compiler/emit/c_backend.orb:2082`), and `branch` splits the arm
// from the next test. An arm that returns writes no jump; a block arm jumps to the
// merge block, which is also where the wildcard lives -- the last test's false side
// IS the wildcard's block, so no empty block is printed for it.
import "../../compiler/tir/entry.orb"

fn main() {
    val source = "enum Color { Red Green }  fn code(c: Color) -> int { match c { Color.Red => { print(1) } Color.Green => { print(2) } _ => { print(3) } } return 0 }"
    print(compileFrontendSource("fixture", source))
}
